WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.180
Two pea plants both have purple flowers.

2
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:13.860
Both carry one purple allele and one white allele. Most of their offspring, their

3
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.580
young plants, are predicted to bloom purple.

4
00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.280
About one in four is predicted to bloom white.

5
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.600
Where was the white hiding?

6
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.120
Could we predict it before planting a single seed?

7
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:32.143
Each body cell of a pea plant carries two copies of the flower color gene.

8
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:35.423
An allele is one version of a gene.

9
00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:39.323
Pea plants have a purple allele and a white allele.

10
00:00:40.523 --> 00:00:43.263
Scientists write them as capital P and small p.

11
00:00:43.903 --> 00:00:47.163
A dominant allele shows with only one copy.

12
00:00:47.863 --> 00:00:50.763
A recessive allele shows only with both copies.

13
00:00:51.623 --> 00:00:54.303
The small p is hidden, not destroyed.

14
00:00:55.217 --> 00:00:59.097
The pair of alleles a plant carries is its genotype.

15
00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:02.057
The trait that shows is its phenotype.

16
00:01:03.077 --> 00:01:06.357
Two purple alleles, or one of each, look purple.

17
00:01:07.257 --> 00:01:09.557
Only two white alleles look white.

18
00:01:10.557 --> 00:01:12.737
Each sex cell carries one allele.

19
00:01:13.437 --> 00:01:18.317
A plant with one of each, Pp, passes on either allele, with a 50 percent chance each.

20
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:24.847
A Punnett square is a grid of every way two parents' sex cells can join.

21
00:01:25.787 --> 00:01:30.247
Write one parent's sex cells across the top, and the other's down the side.

22
00:01:31.087 --> 00:01:33.047
Fill each box with its two letters.

23
00:01:33.907 --> 00:01:36.267
Each box has a one in four chance.

24
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:45.960
Read the boxes. One has two purple alleles, two have one of each, and one has two

25
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:52.440
white alleles. A ratio compares two numbers: three purple to one white.

26
00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.160
Probability is the chance something will happen.

27
00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.400
White needs both parents to pass on the white allele.

28
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.680
That chance is one in four, or 25 percent.

29
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:10.520
The square gives the chance for each offspring, not an exact count.

30
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:15.900
Four seeds from two parents with one of each, Pp, might all bloom purple.

31
00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.660
The chance of that is about 32 percent. Mendel counted thousands of pea seeds.

32
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:30.120
His seed shape counts came out about 2.96 to 1, close to 3 to 1 but not exact.

33
00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:34.900
So we can predict the chances, and real counts land near them.
